menu "SenseThrough single-ESP32 CSI"

choice WISP_LINK_MODE
    prompt "Link mode (where the transmitted frames come from)"
    default WISP_LINK_MODE_STA
    help
        One ESP32 measures CSI only on frames it RECEIVES, so pick what transmits:

        STA     - join an existing Wi-Fi network; the ROUTER is the transmitter.
                  Traffic comes from the laptop (scripts/traffic.py, exact rate) and/or
                  the board pinging its own gateway. Recommended.
        SOFTAP  - the board is the access point; a laptop/phone joins it and sends the
                  traffic. No router or credentials needed.
        SNIFFER - fully passive on one channel; CSI from beacons already on the air
                  (~10 Hz, not under our control). No association at all.

config WISP_LINK_MODE_STA
    bool "STA — join an existing network (router is the transmitter)"

config WISP_LINK_MODE_SOFTAP
    bool "SoftAP — the board is the AP, a laptop joins and sends traffic"

config WISP_LINK_MODE_SNIFFER
    bool "Sniffer — passive, CSI from whatever is on air"

endchoice

config WISP_WIFI_SSID
    string "STA: SSID to join"
    default "myrouter"
    depends on WISP_LINK_MODE_STA

config WISP_WIFI_PASSWORD
    string "STA: password"
    default "mypassword"
    depends on WISP_LINK_MODE_STA

config WISP_SOFTAP_SSID
    string "SoftAP: SSID to publish"
    default "sensethrough"
    depends on WISP_LINK_MODE_SOFTAP

config WISP_SOFTAP_PASSWORD
    string "SoftAP: password (blank or <8 chars = open network)"
    default "sensethrough"
    depends on WISP_LINK_MODE_SOFTAP

config WISP_SOFTAP_CHANNEL
    int "SoftAP: channel"
    range 1 13
    default 6
    depends on WISP_LINK_MODE_SOFTAP

config WISP_SNIFFER_CHANNEL
    int "Sniffer: channel to sit on"
    range 1 13
    default 6
    depends on WISP_LINK_MODE_SNIFFER
    help
        Must match the channel of the AP you want to hear. Check your router settings.

config WISP_MAC_FILTER
    string "Only emit CSI from this transmitter MAC (blank = auto)"
    default ""
    help
        CSI from several transmitters mixed into one signal is meaningless — each sender
        is a different radio channel. In STA/SoftAP mode this is set automatically to the
        peer we associated with, so leave it blank. In SNIFFER mode set it to one AP BSSID
        (aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff), otherwise every AP in range pollutes the motion signal.

config WISP_CSI_LLTF_ONLY
    bool "LLTF subcarriers only (constant width, 1/3 the UART bytes)"
    default y
    help
        Strongly recommended. LLTF-only CSI has a CONSTANT subcarrier count, which is what
        the fixed-width room mask wants, and it cuts each serial line to about a third —
        the UART, not the radio, is what caps the packet rate. Enable HT-LTF only if you
        specifically want the extra subcarriers and have the baud budget for them.

config WISP_UDP_PORT
    int "UDP sink port (where scripts/traffic.py aims)"
    range 1024 65535
    default 8888
    help
        The board binds this port and discards whatever arrives. The payload is irrelevant:
        each datagram is one received Wi-Fi frame, i.e. one CSI sample. This is what makes
        the packet rate a number you choose instead of one you hope for.

config WISP_SELF_PING
    bool "Also ping the gateway to generate traffic without the laptop"
    default y
    depends on WISP_LINK_MODE_STA
    help
        Each echo reply is a frame from the router to us, i.e. one CSI sample — so the
        board still streams CSI when nothing else is running. Routers rate-limit ICMP, so
        this is a floor, not a substitute for the UDP generator.

config WISP_PING_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Self-ping interval (ms)"
    range 10 1000
    default 30
    help
        30 ms is about 33 Hz if the router keeps up. Below ~20 ms most routers start
        dropping or coalescing replies.

config WISP_QUEUE_DEPTH
    int "CSI queue depth (packets)"
    range 4 64
    default 16
    help
        Buffers CSI between the Wi-Fi callback and the printing task. Deeper rides out UART
        hiccups at the cost of RAM (~400 bytes each) and of latency when it is full.

config WISP_STAT_INTERVAL_S
    int "CSI_STAT diagnostic interval (s)"
    range 1 60
    default 5

endmenu
